(ns kekkai.funnel
  "kekkai funnel — public internet ingress to a service on a NAT'd tailnet node,
  the Tailscale-Funnel equivalent. Pure definitions only (no store, no I/O), so
  the TailnetGovernor, the coord-LLM and the netmap projector all read the same
  rules without the censor sharing code with the proposer.

  ## What a funnel is, in this control plane's terms

  A funnel is a GRANT, not a tunnel. It says: the public DNS name `host` may be
  answered by `edge` (a node that can accept inbound internet connections), and
  `edge` may carry those connections across the overlay to `service` on `node`
  at `port`. The control plane records that grant and publishes it in exactly
  two netmaps — the edge's and the target's — together with a `:funnel`
  capability edge `edge → node` scoped to that one port. The nodes open the
  listener and the sessions; the actor never carries a packet (charter G1).

  It is also the only grant in this system whose far side is *not a node*. Every
  other edge joins two keys the control plane admitted; a funnel joins one of
  them to the entire internet. That is why exposing one is high-stakes — always
  a human call, the same class as approving an exit node, which is the other
  place traffic crosses between the tailnet and the public network — and why
  its invariants are hard (un-overridable) rather than advisory.

  ## Why `:funnel` is its own capability

  `:private-http` means 'this peer may speak HTTP to that peer inside the
  overlay'. A funnel edge means 'traffic that originated on the public internet
  may be forwarded along this edge'. The same port and the same pair of nodes
  carry different risk under the two readings, so a node that already grants
  `:private-http` to its edge must NOT thereby have agreed to receive internet
  traffic — and a funnel must never widen what an ordinary peer may do. Two
  capabilities keep the two decisions apart.

  ## What is never recorded

  Charter G4: the control plane authorizes reachability; it does not observe
  what flows through it. A funnel record carries the binding (host, edge, node,
  service, port) and nothing else — no request log, no client addresses, no hit
  counts. There is no field for them, so there is nothing to leak or subpoena.")

(def edge-tag
  "The tag a node must carry, OWNED per its tailnet's `:tag-owners`, to run a
  funnel's public listener.

  An edge holds an internet-facing socket, which no other node in a tailnet is
  expected to do, so being eligible is a designation an organization makes on
  purpose — through the same tag-ownership model that already stops a node from
  self-escalating into `tag:server`. A node that merely *claims* the tag, with
  an owner the policy does not authorize for it, is exactly the self-escalation
  that model exists to refuse."
  "tag:funnel-edge")

(def capability
  "The edge capability a funnel conveys. Distinct from `:private-http` — see
  the namespace docstring."
  :funnel)

(defn spec
  "The funnel a request asks for, as `{:host :edge :node :service :port}`.

  Taken from the REQUEST (the operator's intent), never from the coord-LLM's
  proposal: the governor censors this map and the commit writes this map, so
  there is no path by which an advisor can retarget a funnel between the check
  and the write."
  [request]
  (select-keys request [:host :edge :node :service :port]))

(def ^:private label
  ;; RFC 1123 host label, lowercase only: 1-63 chars, alnum at both ends.
  "[a-z0-9](?:[a-z0-9-]{0,61}[a-z0-9])?")

(def ^:private host-re
  ;; two or more labels, no trailing dot. One regex rather than a split, so the
  ;; check reads the same on the JVM and on the kbb engine (a JS `split` takes
  ;; a string separator literally).
  (re-pattern (str "(?:" label "\\.)+" label)))

(defn host-problem
  "Why `host` is not an acceptable public DNS name for a funnel, or nil.

  Lowercase, no port, no trailing dot, at least two labels, ≤253 chars, every
  label an RFC 1123 label. Three refusals are the point rather than pedantry:

  - **No IP literal.** A funnel is addressed by a name so that it can be moved
    between edges and withdrawn by withdrawing the name. An IPv4 literal passes
    a naive label check (`203.0.113.7` is four numeric labels), so a name whose
    top-level label is all digits is refused; IPv6 fails on `:`.
  - **No wildcard.** `*.example.net` would bind every name under a zone to one
    service, including names nobody has thought of yet.
  - **Exactly one spelling.** `Blocks.Example.NET.` and `blocks.example.net`
    are the same DNS name. Accepting both would let two funnels bind one name
    past the uniqueness check, and it would make the signed netmap depend on
    how someone typed it. Canonical form is required, not normalized, so what
    was approved is byte-for-byte what is published."
  [host]
  (cond
    (not (string? host)) :not-a-string
    (> (count host) 253) :too-long
    (not (re-matches host-re host)) :invalid-name
    (re-matches #"[0-9]+" (re-find #"[^.]+$" host)) :ip-literal))

(defn local?
  "A LOCAL funnel: the edge IS the target (`:edge` = `:node`). The node is
  itself publicly reachable (e.g. `gad` over IPv6) and serves its own service
  straight from its public listener — nothing crosses the overlay, so a local
  funnel grants no node-to-node reachability at all: no `:funnel` edge, no
  peer. A REMOTE funnel (edge ≠ node) forwards over the overlay to a NAT'd
  target and carries both. The same node can be the local funnel for its own
  service and the edge of remote funnels for others."
  [{:keys [edge node]}]
  (and (some? edge) (= edge node)))

(defn valid-port? [port]
  (and (int? port) (<= 1 port 65535)))
